          "text": "     1→# use-ai High-Level Feature Requirements & Test Coverage\n     2→\n     3→This document lists user-facing capabilities in requirements format with test coverage status.\n     4→\n     5→**Legend:**\n     6→- ✅ TESTED - Feature has test coverage\n     7→- 🆗 OK - Not explicitly tested, but OK (implicitly tested or obvious by existence)\n     8→- ❌ NOT TESTED - Feature exists but lacks tests\n     9→- ⚠️ PARTIAL - Feature has some test coverage but gaps remain\n    10→\n    11→---\n    12→\n    13→## Client Features\n    14→\n    15→### Tool Definition & Registration\n    16→\n    17→✅ React components can expose tools (functions) to the AI using the useAI hook\n    18→✅ Tools can be defined with type-safe parameters using Zod schemas\n    19→✅ Tools can be defined without parameters\n    20→✅ Tools can be marked as requiring confirmation before execution\n    21→✅ Tool execution errors are caught and reported back to the AI\n    22→✅ Tools are automatically registered when components mount and unregistered when they unmount\n    23→\n    24→### Component State Management\n    25→\n    26→✅ Components can provide their current state to the AI via the prompt option\n    27→✅ The system waits for React re-renders after tool execution to capture updated state before responding\n    28→✅ Components can be marked as \"invisible\" to skip render waiting (for provider-type components)\n    29→✅ Multiple instances of the same component can register separate tools using unique IDs\n    30→✅ Tool names are automatically namespaced when component IDs are provided\n    31→\n    32→### Chat & Conversation Management\n    33→\n    34→✅ Chat history is automatically persisted to localStorage by default\n    35→✅ Users can create multiple chats and switch between them\n    36→✅ Chat titles are auto-generated from the first user message\n    37→✅ A maximum of 20 chats are stored by default, with oldest chats auto-deleted\n    38→✅ Users can delete individual chats from the history\n    39→✅ Chat messages persist across page reloads\n    40→✅ Full conversation context is maintained when resuming chats\n    41→✅ Messages support displayMode metadata for custom styling ('default' | 'error')\n    42→✅ Error messages are visually distinguished with light red backgrounds and red text\n    43→✅ Display modes are persisted with messages and restored on page reload\n    44→✅ Custom chat storage backends can be implemented via the ChatRepository interface\n    45→\n    46→### User Interface\n    47→\n    48→✅ A floating button provides access to the AI chat interface\n    49→✅ The floating button indicates connection status (green when connected, gray when offline)\n    50→✅ The floating button shows an unread indicator when new messages arrive\n    51→✅ A chat panel displays conversation history with timestamps\n    52→✅ The chat panel supports multi-line input with Shift+Enter for newlines\n    53→✅ The chat panel shows a \"Thinking...\" indicator while the AI processes requests\n    54→✅ Empty chat displays up to 4 randomly selected suggestions from all mounted components\n    55→✅ Users can click suggestions to send them as messages\n    56→❌ Custom UI components can replace the default floating button\n    57→❌ Custom UI components can replace the default chat panel\n    58→✅ The chat UI can be completely disabled by passing null to CustomButton and CustomChat props\n    59→\n    60→### Connection & Error Handling\n    61→\n    62→✅ The client automatically connects to the WebSocket server on initialization\n    63→✅ The client automatically reconnects with exponential backoff on disconnect\n    64→✅ Connection status is exposed to components via the useAI hook\n    65→✅ The system prevents sending messages when disconnected\n    66→✅ Error messages are displayed in the chat UI with distinctive red styling (light red bubble, red text)\n    67→✅ Error messages use a configurable ErrorMapping system for internationalization (i18n)\n    68→✅ Three error types are supported: API_OVERLOADED, RATE_LIMITED, UNKNOWN_ERROR\n    69→✅ Default English error messages are provided for all error types\n    70→✅ Custom error messages can be configured via the errorMessages prop on UseAIProvider\n    71→✅ Error codes are defined in an exhaustive enum shared between server and client\n    72→✅ Error messages persist across page reloads with displayMode metadata\n    73→❌ Custom error handlers can be provided via the onError callback\n    74→\n    75→### Context & System Prompts\n    76→\n    77→❌ A global system prompt can be configured to provide instructions to the AI\n    78→❌ The useAIContext hook provides access to connection state and chat management functions\n    79→   NOTE: The hook exists and chat management functions are tested via chat-management.integration.test.tsx,\n    80→   but the hook itself is not directly tested\n    81→\n    82→### MCP Integration (Client-Side)\n    83→\n    84→❌ Custom headers can be provided for MCP requests via the mcpHeadersProvider\n    85→❌ The mcpHeadersProvider function is called on each AI invocation to get fresh headers\n    86→❌ MCP header providers support exact URL matching and glob patterns\n    87→   NOTE: Client-side MCP feature exists in implementation but has no test coverage\n    88→\n    89→---\n    90→\n    91→## Server Features\n    92→\n    93→### Core Architecture\n    94→\n    95→✅ The server coordinates communication between client applications and AI agents using WebSocket (Socket.IO)\n    96→✅ The AG-UI protocol is used for communication between client and server\n    97→✅ The server maintains separate sessions for each connected client\n    98→✅ The server tracks conversation history and tool calls per session\n    99→✅ The server exposes a /health endpoint for Kubernetes health checks\n   100→\n   101→### Agent System\n   102→\n   103→✅ Multiple AI agents can be configured (Claude, OpenAI, Google, etc. via AI SDK)\n   104→✅ The AISDKAgent integrates with any AI SDK language model\n   105→✅ Agents automatically handle multi-step tool execution (up to 10 steps)\n   106→✅ Custom agents can be implemented by implementing the Agent interface\n   107→⚠️ The system instructs the AI to ask for confirmation before calling confirmation-required tools\n   108→\n   109→### Tool Execution Coordination\n   110→\n   111→✅ The AI can run MCP tools to do tasks in the frontend\n   112→✅ The server coordinates tool calls between the AI and client\n   113→✅ The server waits for tool results from the client before continuing\n   114→✅ The server handles multiple sequential tool calls in a single conversation turn\n   115→\n   116→### Rate Limiting\n   117→\n   118→✅ Rate limiting can be configured per IP address using a sliding window algorithm\n   119→✅ The maximum number of requests per window can be configured via environment variables\n   120→✅ The window duration can be configured via environment variables\n   121→✅ Rate limiting can be disabled by setting max requests to 0\n   122→✅ Different clients have independent rate limits\n   123→✅ Rate limits reset after the time window expires\n   124→✅ The system returns helpful error messages with retry-after information when rate limited\n   125→\n   126→### MCP (Model Context Protocol) Integration\n   127→\n   128→✅ Remote MCP servers can be specified on the backend to provide additional tools\n   129→✅ Each MCP endpoint specifies a URL, optional headers, optional namespace prefix, and timeout\n   130→✅ MCP tool names are prefixed with namespace to avoid conflicts\n   131→✅ The server fetches MCP tool schemas on initialization using JSON-RPC 2.0\n   132→✅ The server retries failed MCP endpoint initialization up to 3 times\n   133→✅ MCP tools are executed via JSON-RPC 2.0 calls to the remote server\n   134→✅ MCP tool execution has configurable timeout (default 30 seconds)\n   135→⚠️ Custom headers can be added to MCP requests (server-wide)\n   136→⚠️ Custom headers can be added to MCP requests (per-request)\n   137→⚠️ Per-request headers override server-wide configured headers\n   138→✅ MCP header configuration supports exact URL matching and glob patterns\n   139→✅ MCP tool schemas can be periodically refreshed when configured\n   140→✅ MCP endpoints are cleaned up when the server shuts down\n   141→\n   142→### Plugin Architecture\n   143→\n   144→⚠️ The server supports plugins that extend functionality\n   145→⚠️ Plugins can register custom message handlers for new message types\n   146→⚠️ Plugins receive lifecycle hooks when clients connect and disconnect\n   147→⚠️ Plugins have access to the client session for state management\n   148→\n   149→### Observability & Logging\n   150→\n   151→✅ Structured logging is available in JSON or pretty-printed format\n   152→✅ Log format can be configured via LOG_FORMAT environment variable\n   153→✅ The logger supports info, warn, error, and debug levels\n   154→✅ The logger includes timestamps in all log entries\n   155→✅ The logger redacts sensitive header values in MCP logs (unless DEBUG=1)\n   156→✅ Langfuse observability can be enabled for LLM tracking and analytics\n   157→✅ Langfuse integration tracks conversation sessions, tool calls, and token usage\n   158→✅ Langfuse telemetry includes session metadata (sessionId, threadId, runId, ipAddress, toolCount)\n   159→✅ Langfuse observability is automatically enabled when LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY and LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY are set\n   160→\n   161→### Error Handling\n   162→\n   163→✅ The server emits RUN_ERROR events when agent execution fails\n   164→✅ The server catches and handles AI SDK model errors\n   165→✅ The server detects API overload errors (HTTP 529) and sends API_OVERLOADED error code\n   166→✅ The server detects rate limiting errors (HTTP 429) and sends RATE_LIMITED error code\n   167→✅ The server sends structured error codes using the ErrorCode enum (not raw error messages)\n   168→✅ The server logs detailed error information